  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nSay you write some HTML like this:\n\n```\n<html>\n<head>\n\t<style>\n\t\tdetails.inline, details.inline summary {\n\t\t\tdisplay: inline;\n\t\t}\n\t</style>\n</head>\n\n<body>\n\t<p>Here is an <details class=\"inline\"><summary>inline details block</summary> that you can click on and expand!</details></p>\n</body>\n\n</html>\n```\n\nSeems simple enough, right?\n\nHowever, every browser I have tried to do this in refuses to render it the way I expect. The content of the `details` tag is, nonetheless, rendered as a separate block.\n\nIf I view the raw page source, sure, everything looks fine; but if I use the developer tools in my browser to inspect, it's rendered like this:\n\n<img width=\"465\" height=\"239\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c9d197f9-8d47-4773-9d5e-44778b2e7f45\" />\n\nSince this is universal in every browser, I'm suspecting the problem is something in the HTML specification itself.\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nJavascript, I guess?\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\nAllow `details` elements to be rendered inline.\n\n### Anything else?\n\n_No response_",
